  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1220 AM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nCoolest temperatures this season are expected later this morning \nwith lows in the upper 40s to 50s across interior portions of S TX \nand around 60 to mid 60s across the barrier islands. Cool \ntemperatures, light winds and increasing clouds are forecast through \nthe afternoon, then a gradual warming trend is expected through the \nupcoming week. Temperatures are forecast to be above normal by the \nmiddle of the week as a mid/upper level ridge begins to build across \nS TX.\n\nAs for rain chances, moisture is forecast to increase across the \narea as winds become more onshore. PWATs are progged to increase \nto 1.5-1.9 inches through Monday. This moisture combined with a \nmid level short wave tracking northeast across S TX will bring a \nlow chance of showers to the coastline south of Port Aransas by \nMonday, with higher chances over the coastal waters. However, most\nareas across S TX will remain dry. Low chances continue Tuesday \nalong the coast then spread west across the southern Coastal Bend \nto the southern Brush Country on Wednesday. Models indicate mid \nlevel ridging becomes dominate and PWATs decrease to less than 1 \ninch the latter part of the work week and into next weekend. Thus,\nlittle to no significant rain is expected Thursday and beyond. \n\nModels indicate that a northeast to east wind will persist across \nthe gulf waters through Thursday. Although the flow will generally \nbe 5-15 knots, the persistent direction and swell periods around 8 \nseconds could lead to minor coastal flooding during times of high \ntide. P-ETSS indicates that tide levels will remain below the 2ft \nMSL threshold, thus confidence is low, but will continue to monitor \ntrends.",
